ffset="0" stop-color="#3DD9C7" stop-opacity="0.28"></stop><stop offset="1" stop-color="#3DD9C7" stop-opacity="0"></stop></linearGradient></defs><polygon points="6,55 6,50 47.6,46.17391304347826 89.2,38.24844720496894 130.8,28.13664596273292 172.4,17.47826086956522 214,6 214,55" fill="url(#sp-cash-g)"></polygon><polyline points="6,50 47.6,46.17391304347826 89.2,38.24844720496894 130.8,28.13664596273292 172.4,17.47826086956522 214,6" fill="none" stroke="#3DD9C7" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polyline><circle cx="214" cy="6" r="4" fill="#3DD9C7" stroke="#F1F4F7" stroke-width="2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle></svg><span class="panel-stat__foot">collected across all sources</span></div></div><div class="momentum__foot"><span class="k">Traced to LexCollect</span><strong class="v">every dollar</strong><span class="s">each payment carries its origin, message and outcome</span></div></figure></div><p class="report__caption">Illustrative six-month trend. In the product this view is your firm&#x27;s live data.</p></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="split split--wide-left"><div><p class="eyebrow">Our story</p><h2>The receivables problem, from the inside</h2><p>The firm ran MyCase, then Filevine, took payments through LawPay, tracked leads in HubSpot and closed the books in QuickBooks. Each system was right about its own piece. None of them agreed about the whole, and the &quot;true AR&quot; lived in a spreadsheet someone rebuilt from raw exports every month.</p><p>So we built the thing in the middle. It reconciled a $21M book to the firm&#x27;s own ground truth, found hundreds of contracts marked paid that weren&#x27;t, re-linked $3.5M of balances to the right clients, and replaced rounds of calls with reconciled lists, automated email and SMS, and a record of every contact.</p><p>Then the escalations moved out of email and into queues with owners. Then the partners got a certified number every month. Then other firms asked for it. That&#x27;s LexCollect.</p></div><blockquote class="pullquote">The partners didn&#x27;t need a prettier report. They needed one number they could trust, and a way to reconnect with the clients behind it.<cite>Why LexCollect exists</cite></blockquote></div></div></section><section class="section section--deep section--rule"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">What we care about</p><h2>Eight outcomes, not eight features</h2><p class="lead">Every part of the platform exists to move one of these. If it doesn&#x27;t, we don&#x27;t build it.</p></div><div class="grid grid--4"><div class="card"><span class="kicker">01</span><h3>Maximize revenue</h3><p>Collect more of what was already billed, add paying clients, and see which products to sell more of.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">02</span><h3>Financial visibility</h3><p>One reconciled, certified view of receivables across every system.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">03</span><h3>Recover hard debt</h3><p>A prioritized queue, sequences instead of call rounds, promises that get followed, and failed cards handled gently.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">04</span><h3>Accountability</h3><p>Every team member measured from the full record, every day.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">05</span><h3>Internal communication</h3><p>Escalations with queues, priorities and owners instead of email chains.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">06</span><h3>Client trust</h3><p>The connection between firm and client, rebuilt: nobody is chased for money they already paid.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">07</span><h3>Products &amp; lifecycle</h3><p>Cash by product and where in an engagement&#x27;s life it arrives.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">08</span><h3>Works with your CRM</h3><p>Compatible with any CRM that exports or has an API. Leads validated by money, and a client 360 the books agree with.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="section section--rule"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Principles</p><h2>How we work</h2></div><div class="grid grid--2"><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Principle 01</span><h3>Reconcile before you collect</h3><p>A message built on unreconciled data wastes the firm&#x27;s credibility and the client&#x27;s trust. We tie every dollar to a client, contract and invoice before anyone is contacted.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Principle 02</span><h3>Every finding has a price and an owner</h3><p>A cause, a record count, a dollar figure and a name. If we can&#x27;t put a price on a problem, we say so instead of guessing.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Principle 03</span><h3>Measure people from the full record</h3><p>Scorecards come from every logged contact, not a sample. The first firm&#x27;s old dashboard showed 8% of real activity. Ours certifies the whole thing.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Principle 04</span><h3>Trust comes from checking</h3><p>Cleanups verify themselves against ground truth. Payments can&#x27;t book twice. Every merge is audited and reversible. A green status means the job really ran.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="section section--deep section--rule"><div class="container"><div class="split split--center"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who we serve</p><h2>Firms past the point of keeping receivables in someone&#x27;s head</h2><p class="lead">Mid-size and growing firms with payment plans, multiple systems and a collections function, but without a finance department to chase every dollar full time.</p><p class="muted">The first deployment was a firm with thousands of installment plans and payers who weren&#x27;t the client, which is the hardest version of the problem. The system fits any firm that bills.</p><div class="actions"